<section id="faq" class="odd-sections">
    <div class="container-fluid d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center">
      <div class="container text-center">
        <h2 class="brand-text display-2 mb-4 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <!-- <p class="lead text-start">Here are a few pointers to help you understand what to expect ...</p> -->
        <div class="accordion accordion-flush text-dark text-start" id="questions">

          <!-- Item 1 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-one">
                1. When, Where and What time does the group meet up?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-one" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>We are based in and around the Fleet, Hampshire area of the UK.</p>
                <p>Off-road, Cross Country (XC), Adventure, Exploring riding for fun, easy-paced (average 8-10mph), no
                  racing!</p>
                <p>Most rides (3 out of 4 Saturdays) depart from the Reading Road Canal Wharf car park at 09:30 sharp.
                </p>
                <p>Regular Meet Point: <a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/Rz6o6LjYwvr6974ZA" target="_blank"
                    class="brand-text">Google Maps <i class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right brand-text link-icon"> </i></a>
                  (Longer Rides may differ- see <a href="#ride-diary" class="brand-text">Ride Diary</a>)
                </p>
                <p>Depart time : 09:30 (Longer Rides may differ- see <a href="#ride-diary" class="brand-text">Ride
                    Diary</a>)</p>
                <p>Regular Rides are 1.5-2 hours riding time and are only notified via the <a
                    href="https://chat.whatsapp.com/LJ1XZhB7KprApZhr4plhGD" target="_blank" role="button"
                    class="brand-text">WhatsApp Group <i class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right brand-text link-icon">
                    </i></a>.</p>
                <p>We also aim to have a social meet up on the Tuesday evening after each longer ride, again all
                  details
                  are shared via the <a href="https://chat.whatsapp.com/LJ1XZhB7KprApZhr4plhGD" target="_blank"
                    role="button" class="brand-text">WhatsApp Group <i
                      class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right brand-text link-icon"> </i></a> only.</p>
                <p>There are NO membership fees to join, NO charges for a ride.</p>
                <p>YES, IT IS TOTALLY FREE TO RIDE WITH US!</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 2 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-two">
                2. What sort of bike do I need to enjoy these rides?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-two" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                Bike types:
                <ul>
                  <li>Mountain (MTB)</li>
                  <li>Gravel</li>
                  <li>E-bike <sup class="text-info">**</sup></li>
                  <li>Hybrid</li>
                  <li>Cyclocross</li>
                  <li>Road - possible for skilled riders with appropriate tyres!</li>
                </ul>
                <p>NOTE: Please wear a correctly fitted cycle helmet and have a Bell fitted, we ride on towpaths and other areas where there are walkers/joggers
                etc… a bell is the best way to alert them.</p>
                <p>Depending on the time of year and the length of the Longer Rides, it is recommended to have a set
                  of front and rear lights and certainly use front and rear mudguards when it's wet/muddy... so most of
                  the year! LOL</p>

                <blockquote class="text-info fs-6">
                  <sup class="text-info">**</sup> E-bike riders - Please consider the following:<br />
                  <ul>
                    <li>UK legal E-bikes only</li>
                    <li>Your battery charge at start of the ride.</li>
                    <li>Your battery range.</li>
                    <li>If you plan to use a train to return, please check that the bike will be allowed on the train.</li>
                    <li>Please understand that if you run out of battery charge, it is down to you to get home. </li>
                </ul>
                </blockquote>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 3 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-three">
                3. What sort of tyres do I need?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-three" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>Tyres are a very personal choice, please understand that most of the time we are NOT on tarmac.</p>
                <p>For those new to off road riding, tyres that are more designed for off road riding will inspire
                  more
                  confidence...eg Mountain/Gravel knobbly style tyres</p>
                For more experienced riders they tend to know what they can ride on.
                <p>A point to note, when in the autumn/winter/spring periods ...there is likely to be more mud, hence
                  knobbly tyres give more grip!</p>
                As always, if in doubt, please ask in the <a href="https://chat.whatsapp.com/LJ1XZhB7KprApZhr4plhGD"
                  target="_blank" class="brand-text">WhatsApp Group <i
                    class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right brand-text link-icon"> </i></a> chat.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 4 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-four">
                4. I don't have a suitable bike, how can I give this a go?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-four" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>We usually have 2 loan bikes available. Please ask and we can arrange for you to use one of these
                  and
                  ensure it is the best fit possible for you.</p>
                <p>There is no charge to borrow a bike, we want more people to join us!</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 5 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-five">
                5. What equipment do I need to have with my bike?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-five" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>We recommend that you also bring the following:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>Bell</li>
                  <li>Multitool</li>
                  <li>Spare inner tube(s)</li>
                  <li>Pump</li>
                  <li>Tyre levers</li>
                  <li>Inner tube patches</li>
                </ul>
                <p>NOTE: Please wear a correctly fitted cycle helmet and have a Bell fitted, we ride on towpaths and other areas where there are
                  walkers/joggers
                  etc… a bell is the best way to alert them.</p>
                <p>Depending on the time of year and the length of the Longer Rides, it is recommended to have a set
                  of
                  front and rear lights and certainly use front and rear mudguards when it's wet/muddy... so most of
                  the
                  year! LOL</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 6 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-six">
                6. Do I need any special clothing?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-six" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>To start with… absolute no. Wear what you are comfortable in.</p>
                <p>To improve comfort and safety, consider:
                <ul>
                  <li>A helmet - riding off road there are often low branches, low bridges and other hazards. We insist that a helmet is worn for all rides. If you need to borrow one, we might be able to help. Commonsense says WEAR A HELMET</li>
                  <li>Padded cycle shorts - improves comfort on rides over an hour.</li>
                  <li>Padded cycling gloves - reduces vibration in you hands.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p>After that, there is endless choices! </p>
                <p>FYI: Decathlon shop offers good quality kit for sensible prices - nearest one is in Farnborough or on-line</p>
                <p>Our Sponsors over at Berkshire Cycle Co. Crowthorne & Woodley, will always help you out and offer discounts to the group</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 7 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-seven">
                7. What food and drink are recommended for the rides?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-seven" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>Although we plan to stop along the ride at a cafe, it is highly recommended you carry the
                  following:
                </p>
                Fluids - 1 or 2 bottles (as large as will fit on your bike). These can be filled with water, juice, or
                an energy drink. If you don't have room on your bike, consider using a small backpack with a couple of
                bottles or a hydration pack. It is recommended that you need 500ml of water/fluids per hour of
                exercise.
                <p>Also, ensure you top these up along the route when you have an opportunity. Remember, if you start
                  to
                  feel thirsty you are already starting to dehydrate.</p>
                <p>Foods - It is a good idea always to have some form of emergency rations to hand. These can be
                  energy/oat bars or simple things like Jelly Babies or Haribos.</p>
                <p>Note - that well-trained athletes can only carry up to 90 minutes of energy supplies in their
                  bodies
                  and you see them regularly eating and drinking.</p>
                <p>If in doubt just ask in the <a href="https://chat.whatsapp.com/LJ1XZhB7KprApZhr4plhGD"
                    target="_blank" class="brand-text">WhatsApp Group <i
                      class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right brand-text link-icon"> </i></a> chat.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 8 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-eight">
                8. What else should I know about this group?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-eight" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>We are NOT a club, but rather a group of local cyclists who love to get away from the busy roads on
                  our bikes and enjoy the company of other like minded people.</p>
                <p>We stay in touch and reach out to each other via a <a
                    href="https://chat.whatsapp.com/LJ1XZhB7KprApZhr4plhGD" target="_blank" class="brand-text">WhatsApp
                    Group <i class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right brand-text link-icon"> </i></a> - please feel free to
                  join
                  us, and we look forward to seeing you along a towpath soon.</p>
                <p>Additionally we have a once a month social meet in a local pub on a weekday evening for food, drink
                  and a chat. These are held in the week after our Longer Rides, again all arranged via the <a
                    href="https://chat.whatsapp.com/LJ1XZhB7KprApZhr4plhGD" target="_blank" class="brand-text">WhatsApp
                    Group <i class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right brand-text link-icon"> </i></a>.</p>
                <p>NOTE : Please wear a correctly fitted cycle helmet and have a Bell fitted, we ride on towpaths and other areas where there are
                  walkers/joggers
                  etc… a bell is the best way to alert them.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 9 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-nine">
                9. Do you use Strava, or provide GPX files?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-nine" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>NO, we do not use Strava and have no plans to use Strava or publish Strava routes. Our rides are
                  social events, not training rides or races.</p>
                <p>Generally GPX files are not provided as we are typically riding along the towpaths. Only where we
                  feel the need to have GPX files will we supply them.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 10 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-ten">
                10. What sort of riding do you do?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-ten" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>Off-road, Cross Country (XC), Adventure, Exploring riding for fun, easy-paced (average 8 - 10mph)
                  and
                  social.</p>
                <p>NOTE : We do NOT do Bike parks or similiar style rides</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 11 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-eleven">
                11. Do you have female specific advice?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-eleven" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>NOTE: The following articles were found to be helpful by my wife who has only in the last couple of years started cycling regularly - Thank you Edurne.</p>
                <p>For many years, the majority of cycling equipment was designed primarily for men. Because male and female anatomy differ significantly in key areas, women often struggled to find equipment that worked—particularly when it came to saddles.</p>
                <p>Fortunately, women-specific clothing and saddles are now widely available and can significantly improve your riding experience. These designs are tailored to the female frame to help prevent common points of discomfort. For instance, many women's saddles feature a recessed "cut-out" running along the length to relieve soft-tissue pressure.</p>
                <p>There are many resources available to help you find the best setup for your body. For a comprehensive look at managing comfort and choosing the right gear, we recommend taking a look at these practical guides from Total Women Cycling:</p>
                  <a href="https://totalwomenscycling.com/lifestyle/how-to-treat-saddle-sores" target="_blank"
                    role="button" class="brand-text">Saddle Soreness: Everything You Need to Know <i
                      class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right brand-text"></i></a>.<br />
                  <a href="https://totalwomenscycling.com/road-cycling/maintenance/bike-fitting-what-women-need-to-consider-for-a-bike-fit" target="_blank"
                    role="button" class="brand-text">Bike Fitting: What women need to consider for a bike fit <i
                      class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right brand-text"></i></a>.
                <p></p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 12 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-twelve">
                12. Can I use an 'Action cam' or similar to video the rides?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-twelve" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>NO, absolutely NO ride videos are allowed!</p>
                <p>NO Action Cams, NO GoPros, NO Drones, nothing that in anyway might video the rides!</p>
                <p>NO excuses, NO 'flexing' of this  - it is not open to interpretation! </p>
              </div>
            </div>            
          </div>

          <!-- Item 13 -->
          <div class="accordion-item">
            <h2 class="accordion-header">
              <button class="accordion-button collapsed lead brand-text" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                data-bs-target="#question-thirteen">
                13. What if someone wants to organise a ride?
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div id="question-thirteen" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#questions">
              <div class="accordion-body body-text">
                <p>If anyone wants to set up a ride.</p>

                <p>Things to be considered:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>Design the route so that the <strong>MAJORITY</strong> of it is <strong>OFF-ROAD</strong></li>
                  <li>Consider the safety of the group (not just where you fancy riding)</li>
                  <li>The overall distance</li>
                  <li>The ride time</li>
                  <li>Factor in stops every 15-20 mins (add 5-7 mins per stop)</li>
                  <li>Café stop and time to it: if over 2 hours, flag this and remind folks to carry enough fluid
                    (500ml
                    per hour!)</li>
                  <li>Opt-out points for anyone wanting to only do part of the ride, not feeling it, or having a
                    mechanical issue</li>
                  <li>If not a circular route, determine how to get back and how long does it takes</li>
                  <li>Consider other riders and the speed:
                    <ul>
                      <li>We are <strong>definitely NOT racing</strong></li>
                      <li>We are <strong>NOT pushing the pace</strong></li>
                      <li>We have to consider new/introductory riders</li>
                    </ul>
                  </li>
                </ul> Set expectations for all ahead of any ride...there should be minimal/zero surprises for folks
                <p></p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>